WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.667
Word2Vec.

2
00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:04.571
A tiny network is given a pointless job.

3
00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:07.567
The useful part is what it learns by accident.

4
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:11.567
Word2Vec learns a dense vector for every word.

5
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:16.967
It rests on one idea: words that appear in similar contexts have similar meanings.

6
00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:20.467
Here is the fake job, called skip-gram.

7
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:22.567
Take a center word, like flare.

8
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:24.433
Feed it to a tiny network.

9
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.016
Ask it to predict the words around it.

10
00:00:27.016 --> 00:00:28.938
We throw those predictions away.

11
00:00:28.938 --> 00:00:31.400
The learned vectors are the real product.

12
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.378
After training, the geometry shows the result.

13
00:00:35.378 --> 00:00:36.767
Here is the word flare.

14
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:42.600
Flare and CME keep appearing in the same sentences, so training pushes their vectors together.

15
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.567
X-class joins the cluster.

16
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:48.233
A word from a different world, like scrub, ends up far away.

17
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:53.467
The famous result is that vector arithmetic captures relations.

18
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:56.500
King minus man plus woman lands near queen.

19
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.067
Meaning emerged purely from co-occurrence, and this started the embedding era.

20
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.040
There's one key limitation.

21
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.667
Word2Vec is static: one vector per word.

22
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:11.815
So dock the maneuver and dock the harbor structure collapse into the same point.

23
00:01:11.815 --> 00:01:13.633
Context-aware models fix this.

24
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:16.800
Here is the training in a few lines.

25
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.000
Each log line becomes a list of lowercase words.

26
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.533
We train with a window of five, and setting sg to one selects skip-gram.

27
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:29.200
Then we check the neighbors of a few words, to see if the space makes domain sense.

28
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.567
Every embedding model today descends from this idea.

29
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:39.933
A query like sun eruptions this week retrieves X-class flare chunks despite sharing no words.

30
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.234
That is distributional semantics.

31
00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:47.267
And a small Word2Vec on your own logs is a quick check that your corpus has signal.

32
00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:49.191
Let's recap.

33
00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:53.233
Skip-gram predicts neighbors, and the vectors are the real product.

34
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:55.967
Similar contexts produce similar vectors.

35
00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:58.733
Relations show up as vector arithmetic.

36
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:01.790
But the vectors are static, one per word.

37
00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:03.000
Next up: FastText.
